;;; FirstOrderModalLogic.mon --- First-order modal logic as executable mathematics

;;; Commentary:

;; This chapter studies quantified modal logic (QML): classical first-order
;; logic extended by □ (necessity) and ◇ (possibility).  Every displayed result
;; below is produced by the core-owned Logic.FirstOrder.Modal module.

;;; Code:

import Logic.FirstOrder.Modal

module Main


;;; 1. The object language

;; Terms denote individuals.  Formula values express propositions about those
;; individuals.  Here x is a variable, a is a constant, Fx is the atomic
;; formula obtained by applying the unary predicate symbol F to x, and Rxy is a
;; binary predicate application.

define x :: Term (Variable "x")


define a :: Term (Constant "a")


define Fx :: Formula (Predicate "F" (list x))


define Fa :: Formula (Predicate "F" (list a))


define Rxy :: Formula (Predicate "R" (list x (Variable "y")))


;; Mathematical readings:
;;
;;   Fx       F holds of x
;;   x = a    x and a denote the same individual
;;   ¬Fx      F does not hold of x
;;   Fx ∧ Gx  both conjuncts hold
;;   Fx ∨ Gx  at least one disjunct holds


;;; 2. Binding and modal scope

;; ∀ binds every free occurrence of x in its body; ∃ is its existential dual.
;; □A holds when A is true at every accessible world.  ◇A holds when A is true
;; at some accessible world.

define every-individual-is-F :: Formula (∀ "x" Fx)


define some-individual-is-F :: Formula (∃ "x" Fx)


define necessarily-Fx :: Formula (□ Fx)


define possibly-Fx :: Formula (◇ Fx)


;; Scope matters:
;;
;;   ∀x. □Fx    every individual is necessarily F
;;   □∀x. Fx    necessarily, every individual is F
;;
;; These formulas need not coincide when domains vary between worlds.


;;; 3. The Barcan formula

;; The formula studied in this chapter is
;;
;;   ∀x. □Fx -> □∀x. Fx
;;
;; This direction is commonly called the Barcan formula.  Its reverse,
;; □∀x. Fx -> ∀x. □Fx, is commonly called the converse Barcan formula.
;; Naming conventions vary, so quote the formula as well as its name.
;;
;; The core module supports two equivalent notations.  The first is ordinary
;; canonical Monad syntax: every logical connective is an ordinary function.

define barcan-sexp :: Formula
  (implies (∀ "x" (□ Fx)) (□ (∀ "x" Fx)))


;; The second is mathematical surface syntax.  Because the expected type is
;; Formula, the scoped reader exported by Logic.FirstOrder.Modal applies only
;; to this body.  It lowers to the same ordinary function applications as the
;; definition above; there is no modal-logic AST hidden in the compiler.

define barcan-math :: Formula
  ∀x. □Fx -> □∀x. Fx


show render-formula barcan-sexp
show render-formula barcan-math


;;; 4. How the two notations are related

;; The mathematical reader is a precedence parser.  Its declaration lives in
;; Logic.FirstOrder.Modal, beside Formula and its constructors:
;;
;;   ¬, □, ◇       prefix operators (tightest)
;;   ∧              binding power 3, left associative
;;   ∨              binding power 2, left associative
;;   ->             binding power 1, right associative
;;   ∀x. and ∃x.    bind a body at binding power 2
;;
;; Consequently A -> B -> C means A -> (B -> C), while A ∧ B ∨ C means
;; (A ∧ B) ∨ C.  Binding quantifiers at power 2 makes the implication in the
;; displayed Barcan formula the outermost connective:
;;
;;   (∀x. □Fx) -> (□∀x. Fx)
;;
;; This is expected-type-directed syntax.  The notation is enabled for the
;; body of a definition explicitly typed Formula; it cannot reinterpret -> in
;; an Int -> Int type, a pattern clause, or a for binding.  A body headed by a
;; declared target function, such as (implies ...), remains canonical Monad and
;; bypasses mathematical lowering.  Both paths construct precisely the same
;; core Formula values.


;;; 5. Kripke semantics

;; A Kripke frame is a pair (W,R): W is a set of worlds and R is the
;; accessibility relation.  A Kripke model adds a domain of individuals and an
;; interpretation of predicates at each world.
;;
;; The proof below uses constant-domain semantics: every world has the same
;; domain.  Parameters designate rigidly, so the witness a introduced at v is
;; also available for universal instantiation at w.


;;; 6. A labelled analytic tableau

;; To test validity, assume the negation of the Barcan formula at world w and
;; apply truth-preserving tableau rules.  Formula labels name worlds; wRv means
;; that v is accessible from w.
;;
;;   1.  ¬(∀x. □Fx -> □∀x. Fx)   (w)       assumption
;;   2.  ∀x. □Fx                  (w)(1)    ¬ implication, antecedent
;;   3.  ¬□∀x. Fx                 (w)(1)    ¬ implication, ¬ consequent
;;   4.  wRv                      (3)       fresh accessible world
;;   5.  ¬∀x. Fx                  (v)(3)    ¬ necessity
;;   6.  ¬Fa                      (v)(5)    fresh parameter a
;;   7.  □Fa                      (w)(2)    universal instantiation
;;   8.  Fa                       (v)(7,4)  necessity along wRv
;;                                      × 6,8
;;
;; Lines 6 and 8 assert ¬Fa and Fa at the same world.  The branch therefore
;; closes.  Since this example has only one branch, the tableau establishes the
;; formula under the stated constant-domain assumptions.  An open saturated
;; branch would instead determine a countermodel.

show render-barcan-tableau "F"


;;; 7. Vocabulary for further study

;; Search for: object language, term, atomic formula, free and bound variable,
;; substitution, alpha-equivalence, quantified modal logic, Kripke frame,
;; Kripke model, accessibility relation, rigid designation, constant domain,
;; varying domain, increasing domain, decreasing domain, labelled analytic
;; tableau, witness, parameter, branch saturation, branch closure, countermodel,
;; Barcan formula, converse Barcan formula, embedded domain-specific language
;; (EDSL), expected-type-directed syntax, Pratt parser, binding power,
;; associativity, precedence, desugaring, and conservative language extension.
